Installing Quilzo
*****************

Three ways in, in rough order of how much you have to have already: a signed
binary, a container image, or the source.

Until the first release there was only the third, and an INSTALL that says
"have a Go toolchain" is a program with no distribution.  That is fixed as of
v0.1.0 and this file is in the order somebody actually wants.


A binary
========

One static file.  No runtime, no libraries, nothing to install alongside it.
Six platforms: linux, macOS and Windows, each on amd64 and arm64.

     https://github.com/Quilzo/Quilzo/releases/latest

Pick the file for your machine, and — this is the part worth not skipping —
check it before you run it:

     curl -LO https://github.com/Quilzo/Quilzo/releases/latest/download/quilzo-linux-amd64
     curl -LO https://github.com/Quilzo/Quilzo/releases/latest/download/SHA256SUMS
     sha256sum -c --ignore-missing SHA256SUMS

Then put it somewhere on your PATH:

     chmod +x quilzo-linux-amd64
     sudo install -m 0755 quilzo-linux-amd64 /usr/local/bin/quilzo

`quilzo` with no suffix is the same program built for linux/amd64.  It exists
because a release asset called `quilzo` is what a script expects to fetch.


Checking it came from here
--------------------------

A checksum proves the file matches the list.  It does not prove the list came
from this project, and if you fetched both from the same place you have
checked that a page is consistent with itself.

Every release artefact carries a build-provenance attestation binding it to
the workflow run that produced it:

     gh attestation verify quilzo-linux-amd64 --repo Quilzo/Quilzo

That succeeds only if the file was built by this repository's release
workflow, from a tag, and it names which one.  It is a stronger statement than
the checksum and it is one command.


What is in a release besides the binaries
-----------------------------------------

     quilzo.cdx.json      CycloneDX 1.6 bill of materials
     quilzo.crypto.json   cryptographic inventory, with post-quantum status
     SHA256SUMS           for everything above

The bill of materials is generated by the binary rather than read from the
source tree, so it describes what is in the file you downloaded rather than
what would be built from HEAD today.  In v0.1.0 it lists one component: the Go
toolchain.  There is no second entry because there is no dependency.


A container
===========

     docker run --rm -v quilzo:/srv ghcr.io/quilzo/quilzo --root /srv/store init
     docker run --rm -v quilzo:/srv ghcr.io/quilzo/quilzo --root /srv/store demo
     docker run -p 8081:8081 -v quilzo:/srv \
       ghcr.io/quilzo/quilzo --root /srv/store site --addr 0.0.0.0:8081

Then http://127.0.0.1:8081.  `demo` publishes, so there is nothing else to run.

One volume, mounted at `/srv` rather than at the store.  `quilzo demo` writes
templates beside the working directory rather than into the store, so keeping
only `/srv/store` loses them and `site` exits with `no template directory at
templates`.  Both live under `/srv`, so that is what is kept.

This needs v0.2.0 or later.  The v0.1.0 image declared `VOLUME /srv/store`,
which Docker satisfies with a throwaway volume when you mount the parent, so
the store silently vanished between commands; and it shipped no
`/srv/templates` for a volume to be seeded from, so mounting one there was
created owned by root and unwritable by a container running as nonroot, from an
image with no shell to fix it with.  On v0.1.0 the working incantation is
`-v quilzo:/srv -v quilzo-store:/srv/store`.

The base is `gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12:nonroot`: the binary, CA
certificates, a passwd entry and timezone data.  No shell, no package manager,
no interpreter, no libc.  It runs as nonroot.  amd64 and arm64.

The image has the same provenance the binaries do:

     gh attestation verify oci://ghcr.io/quilzo/quilzo:latest --repo Quilzo/Quilzo

Image tags drop the `v`.  The git tag `v0.1.0` publishes `0.1.0`, `0.1` and
`latest`, so `ghcr.io/quilzo/quilzo:v0.1.0` is not a thing that exists and
asking for it gets a 404 indistinguishable from the image being private.  That
is a note rather than a footnote because this file asked for the wrong one
first.


From source
===========

Requirements
------------

Go 1.27 or later.  Nothing else.

This is a Go program with no dependencies: `go.mod` has no require block, so
the standard library and the Go toolchain are the whole build.  There is no
`./configure`, because there is nothing to configure at build time — no
optional libraries to detect and no feature switches.  That is a departure
from the GNU convention and it is a departure with a reason rather than an
omission.


Building and installing
-----------------------

     make
     sudo make install

`make install` honours the usual variables:

     sudo make install prefix=/usr/local     # the default
     make install prefix="$HOME/.local"      # no root needed
     sudo make install DESTDIR=/tmp/stage    # for a package build

To remove it:

     sudo make uninstall


Without make
------------

     go build -o quilzo ./cmd/quilzo
     install -m 0755 quilzo /usr/local/bin/


Checking the build
==================

     make check

That runs the whole test suite: eighty-one packages, including several that
walk this program's own source and fail when a capability exists in one
interface and not the others.

That number is checked rather than remembered.  It said sixty-six until
September 2026, by which point it was wrong by thirteen, and a count in a
document is exactly the kind of claim that goes stale without anybody
noticing — so there is a test that counts the packages and fails naming the
right number.


A first site
============

     quilzo init
     quilzo template use sections
     quilzo add index=templates/sections.json -m "first page"
     quilzo publish
     quilzo site --addr 127.0.0.1:8081


Which licence you have
======================

AGPL-3.0-or-later, unless you have signed a commercial one.  It is not a
trial, it needs no key, and hosting, modifying and charging for this are all
already permitted by it.  See LICENSING.md — the commercial licence exists for
people whose policy prohibits AGPL code, and it covers the same program with
nothing held back.
